course: dictionary
lesson: 15869
title: Unharmed
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unharmed is made of two parts. Un- means not, and harmed means hurt or damaged, so unharmed means not hurt. «All the passengers escaped unharmed.» Escaped tells you there was danger, and unharmed tells you nobody was hurt by it.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «The bus slid off the icy road, but the driver climbed out unharmed.» Slid off the road shows there was a danger, and climbed out shows the driver could move. So unharmed means the driver had no injuries.
explain: Unharmed is about the good outcome after a danger. News reports use it after crashes, fires and rescues to tell readers that everyone is safe. Check your meaning by swapping in "not hurt": if the sentence still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Unharmed means not hurt or damaged, and safe after a danger.
fact: The prefix un- means not, and harm means hurt or damage.
fact: «Unharmed» is often used in news stories after accidents, fires and rescues.
fact: «Unharmed» is the opposite of «injured», which means hurt.
exercise: «The kitten fell into the river, but the rescuers pulled it out unharmed.» What does unharmed mean? | Hungry and very sad | Wet and cold, but still in pain | *Not hurt and safe | Lost forever in the river
exercise: «All the passengers escaped unharmed.» Find and copy the word that tells you everyone was safe. | all | escaped | passengers | *unharmed
exercise: Which word is the opposite of unharmed? | Brave | *Injured | Happy | Fine
test: «The fire spread fast, but every family got out of the house unharmed.» Why does the author use unharmed? | *To show everyone was safe after the danger | To show the house was painted red | To show the family were late | To describe a loud noise
test: Sam says unharmed means "hurt but smiling". What does unharmed really mean? | Hurt but smiling | Lost in a storm | Very, very happy | *Not hurt, and safe after a danger
exam: «After the storm, the sailors reached the shore unharmed.» Which phrase means the same as unharmed? | Badly hurt | Very tired | *Not hurt | Lost at sea
